== 4. phi(x) = 36
ok    solutions of phi(x) = 36 (x <= 3000; phi(x) >= sqrt(x/2) rules out more): [37, 57, 63, 74, 76, 108, 114, 126]
ok    36 is the least n with exactly eight solutions
      Pierpont points with i, j <= 2: [((1, 1), 7), ((1, 2), 19), ((2, 0), 5), ((2, 1), 13), ((2, 2), 37)]
ok    coefficient of X^2 Y^2 in the generating function = 8
ok    table of the eight solutions: factorisations and lattice parts add up to (2,2); counts 1 + 2 + 3 + 2
ok    deg Q(cos 2pi/n) = phi(n)/2 = 2^1 3^2 for the eight n (one square root, two cubic steps); phi(10) = 2^2

== 5. F(n) = sum_{d|n} tau(d)
ok    product formula F(p^a) = T_(a+1) agrees with the definition for n < 400
ok    72 -> 60 -> 54 -> 30 -> 27 -> 10 -> 9 -> 6 -> 9 -> 6
ok    fixed points of F up to 10^6: 1, 3, 18, 36
ok    F(n) > n exactly at 2, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24 (n <= 10^6): six integers
ok    F(n) = 3n/2 exactly at 2, 4, 6, 12 (n <= 10^6): four integers
ok    every orbit from n <= 10^6 ends in 1, 3, 18, 36 or {6, 9}
ok    Liouville at 36: sum tau(d)^3 = 1296 = 36^2
ok    tau_k(p^2 q^2) = T_k^2 = 1^3+..+k^3
      k <= 60 with tau_k(T_k^2) = T_k^2: [1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 13, 22, 37, 46, 58]
ok    k = 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 13, ... giving 36, 100, 225, 441
ok    EDGE: k = 1 also satisfies tau_1(T_1^2) = T_1^2 (tau_1 = 1) although T_1 = 1 is not a product of two primes: the README's Theorem 3 says k >= 2, the page drops it

